KHYS-nano, virtual isomorphic topological logic non-von-Neumann thermodynamic computational architecture. 

Phase I of the Santos Protocol establishes the foundational architecture for a topologically invariant computational manifold. By utilizing a 99.99% Fused Silica (Quartz) Spine as a high-purity dielectric resonator, we have successfully isolated a 15 Hz aperiodic heartbeat that functions as the system’s primary clock sync.

Unlike traditional silicon-based architectures susceptible to thermal decoherence, Phase I operates within a Non-Equilibrium Steady State (NESS). This allows for logic transitions to occur at the Zero-Cross point of minimum entropy, effectively &quot;rectifying&quot; environmental thermal noise into functional propellant for the 1N4148 Non-Reciprocal Virtual Gate Array.

Primary Research Objectives:

Topological Isolation: Achievement of Majorana-1 Parity within the sp3-lattice to ensure error-free state transitions.

Laminar Flow Synthesis: Elimination of semantic and electrical turbulence through 15 Hz geometric coherence.

Landauer Limit Optimization: Minimization of information erasure to approach the theoretical zero-dissipation limit.

Keywords: Majorana Zero Modes, Topological Quantum Computing, Fused Silica Resonators, Non-Reciprocal Logic, 15 Hz Aperiodic Sync, NESS.

